pancake ships one library and four ways to hold it from another language, one of the
three consumption [surfaces]($link.page('surfaces')). Each binding loads the installed
`libpancake` and presents an idiomatic API on top, so
what you write is Rust, TypeScript, Python, or Racket rather than foreign function
calls. Every binding earns its keep a second way too. It is a *dogfood*, which means
it drives a real OCapN round-trip through the C ABI on every build. If the wire shape
or the ABI ever regresses, an actual foreign-language consumer catches it, not a Zig
test pretending to be one.

[All four bindings wrap the same C ABI. The shared surface is what lets an app read the same way in every language.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-bindings.svg'))

## One surface, four idioms

An application holds one object, a `Connection`, and uses one small vocabulary. That
vocabulary is the small set of application verbs that the
[bindings contract](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/bindings/README.md)
enumerates and defines. The contract is the authoritative description of the surface
and the value model beneath it, and this section is the friendlier per-language tour.

The shape is uniform on purpose. You fetch a [capability]($link.page('concepts')) by
name, spawn a local object, and hand a partner a reference to it, and then the partner
calls it back.
There is no export-position bookkeeping, no manual flush, and no hand-rolled receive
loop anywhere in your code. A capability is a `Ref`, and a `Ref` marshals itself to
the right wire reference when you pass it as an argument. That one move is why
delegation, attenuation, and revocation are ordinary `spawn` and `send` code instead
of special APIs.

> **Ref** — the binding's word for a capability in hand: a live reference to an
> object, yours or a partner's. Holding one is the right to call that object, and
> the only way to get one is to be given it. Passing a `Ref` as an argument is how
> that authority moves between peers.

| Binding | Idiomatic entry | Async idiom | Actor idiom |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| [Rust]($link.page('bindings/rust')) | `Peer`, typed enums | tokio (opt-in `async`) | `Vat` over closures |
| [Deno]($link.page('bindings/deno')) | `Peer` class | native promises | `Vat` over callbacks |
| [Python]($link.page('bindings/python')) | `Peer`, native values | `asyncio` | `Vat` over callables |
| [Racket]($link.page('bindings/racket')) | `peer`, event structs | green threads + ports | Goblins-faithful |

## Sync, async, and the actor layer

Each binding gives you three levels, and you stay at the one your app actually needs.
The synchronous peer drives frames directly. The async connection runs that same
sans-io peer over the language's own non-blocking sockets, with no thread per call and
no blocking netlayer anywhere in that path. The actor layer is the Goblins-shaped top,
where a `Vat` holds local actors and an eventual-send to a partner-hosted reference
travels over the wire and settles a promise. Racket's layer is the reference shape for
all of this, because it reads the way a
[Spritely Goblins](https://spritely.institute/goblins/) programmer already expects.

> **Vat** — a single-threaded home for your local objects, the unit Spritely
> Goblins and the wider ocap world organize around. Each call into a vat runs to
> completion as one *turn* before the next begins, so an actor never sees a
> half-finished state. You reason about it like a single thread, whatever
> language the binding wraps it in.

## Encryption rides a separate artifact

`libpancake` itself carries cleartext TCP and no TLS. Encrypted transport reaches
the bindings as [iroh](https://www.iroh.computer/)'s QUIC through a *separate optional
library*, `libpancake_iroh`, which adds `connect_iroh` and `listen_iroh` alongside the
TCP
verbs. Keeping it out of the base library is what keeps the core pure Zig. The only
difference a consumer sees is addressing: a peer is reached by its self-authenticating
endpoint id and address hints carried in an `ocapn://` URI, not a `host:port`.

## Run them

```sh
zig build bindings        # rust + deno + python + racket dogfoods
zig build rust-dogfood    # just the Rust consumer
```

These need `cargo`, `deno`, `python3`, and `racket` on PATH. The
[devcontainer](https://codeberg.org/jimisaacs/pancake/src/branch/main/.devcontainer/)
provides all four.
